SEC8="""## 8. Slow climate states

### 8.1 Candidate states and the engine

The slow states come from free public series, used in two engine-native forms: the level (the engine's tau-day increment is the change over tau days) and the cumulative sum (the increment is the sum of the state over the past tau days, its average). Monthly values are lagged for publication: the Oceanic Nino Index ten days into the second following month, the NCEI drought and degree-day series on the 12th of the following month, other indices on the 15th of the following month, and the NOAA PSL files that update irregularly on the 15th of the second following month. The engine is the author's pairwise CPE engine, unchanged: the probability that a target's forward move over tau_f days lands beyond its q_Y quantile, given that the predictor's past move over tau_p days was beyond its q_X tail, gated at CPE of 0.80, lift of 1.5 against the unconditional rate and 100 observations, with tau from 21 to 300 trading days. An economic prior admits only pre-registered predictor-target channels, each with a one-line mechanism and a confidence tier, and the channels for every batch were registered before its first result. Rows must also pass the episode-independence floor of three separated episodes. Formation runs through 2012, the test is 2013-2026, and thresholds are frozen at their formation values. A survivor filter fixed in advance keeps rows with at least four formation episodes, at least three test episodes with a hit rate of two thirds or more, a test CPE at least ten points above the test base rate and 100 test days.

Four batches were run (Table 1): ENSO, polar and Pacific-decadal indices; observed extremes (corn-belt drought and temperature, gas-weighted heating and power-weighted cooling degree-days, the Indian Ocean Dipole, Atlantic cyclone energy); further slow states (Nino 1+2, 3 and 4, the multivariate ENSO index, tropical North and South Atlantic water, the Atlantic warm pool, the Pacific-North American pattern, the Southern Annular Mode); and rare extremes (drought in the wheat belt, northern plains, Delta, Southeast, Texas and the West, the Trans-Nino index, the North Pacific Gyre Oscillation, global temperature, Northern Hemisphere snow cover and Arctic sea ice). Series with no current free source (the Atlantic multidecadal index, the quasi-biennial oscillation, Indian monsoon rainfall, the Pacific meridional mode) were dropped before any run.

| Batch | Rows passing the gates | At least 3 formation episodes | Claimed CPE | Test CPE against base (lift) | Survivors |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1. ENSO, polar and Pacific-decadal indices (6 series) | 10,352 | 2,461 | 0.870 | 0.458 against 0.464 (0.92) | 43 of 859 |
| 2. Observed extremes (6 series) | 1,228 | 360 | 0.869 | 0.325 against 0.384 (0.86) | 3 of 185 |
| 3. Further slow states (9 series) | 19,738 | 5,142 | 0.876 | 0.498 against 0.471 (1.02) | 181 of 1,932 |
| 4. Rare extremes (11 series) | 8,347 | 1,464 | 0.866 | 0.412 against 0.392 (1.06) | 38 of 567 |
| Control: the program's own market predictors | 18,424 | 4,751 | 0.860 | 0.486 against 0.430 (1.24) | not applicable |

*Table 1. Climate predictors in the CPE engine. Survivors are rows with at least four formation episodes that pass the filter, over all such rows. Test figures use rows with at least 100 test days.*

### 8.2 What the batches claim and what the test shows

Every batch claims a CPE near 0.87 in formation and falls to about the base rate in the test (Table 1); the mean lift is 0.86, 0.92, 1.02 and 1.06 against 1.24 for the program's own predictors, which keep part of their edge. A generic ENSO scan reaches the same verdict in simpler form: """+generic[generic.index("Generalising"):].replace("Generalising, the","the",1)+"""

The survivor filter keeps 43 of 859 rows in the first batch (5.0%), 3 of 185 among the observed extremes, 181 of 1,932 in the third (9.4%) and 38 of 567 in the fourth (6.7%), but their content is thin. In the first batch 41 of the 43 are La Nina-side conditions (Nino 3.4 or ONI in its lower tail) followed by falls in coffee, Korean equities, copper, soybeans and Brazilian and Chinese equities at 126 to 300 days, and none involves sugar. Among the observed extremes the three survivors (heating-degree-day change to natural gas and gasoline at 63 days, cyclone energy to gasoline at 126 days) include no crop; drought and temperature extremes are persistent, so a handful of multi-year episodes supplies the evidence, with 87% of the deepest-5% corn-belt drought days in formation (1963-64, 1976-77, 1988-89, 2012) and only 2022-24 in the test, and the Dipole's change form hits 86% of formation episodes and 18% of test episodes. In the third batch 151 of the 181 survivors come from the Nino regions and the multivariate index, which move together with Nino 3.4; 142 of them are lower-tail conditions followed by falls in the same targets, so they restate the first batch (13 of their 38 target, direction and horizon keys are first-batch keys) and the whole table reduces to 53 distinct keys. The test episodes behind them are few: the five behind Nino 3 and Korean equities are dated 2013-04, 2013-10, 2021-06, 2022-08 and 2023-02, that is, two ENSO phases. The 30 survivors from the Atlantic, Pacific-North American and annular-mode series are the new information (23 keys, 2 shared with the first batch). The tropical North Atlantic carries most of them, but its test episodes (2014-2019) coincide with the commodity bust, when its targets had base rates of 0.71 to 0.74. The cleanest pattern is two-sided: Nino 1+2 in its lower tail precedes a weaker South African rand and in its upper tail a stronger one, and Nino 1+2, the multivariate index and the ONI row of the first batch agree. In the fourth batch the 38 survivors are scattered over 11 predictors and 13 targets, none from the hard-red-winter wheat belt, whose rows score 0.28 in the test against bases of 0.30 and 0.40; the standard-tier mechanism, that drought cuts wheat, does not appear.

### 8.3 A horizon ladder: weather and climate under one rule

The engine's gate cannot compare weather with climate. Run on daily weather predictors with windows of 1 to 28 days and forward horizons of 1 to 21 days, only 5 rows pass the formation gates, against 19,738 for the third climate batch. That contrast is not evidence by itself: a conditional probability of 0.80 for a 1- to 21-day price move is almost unreachable by construction, while the overlapping windows of a 252-day outcome reach it easily, so the gate prejudges the horizon. A fair comparison needs an admission rule that does not.

The ladder applies one rule to both arms. The weather predictors are six degree-day anomaly series (heating and cooling; all, cold-winter, southern, Texas and Midwest metros) over windows of 1, 3, 7, 14, 28, 63, 126 and 252 days; the climate predictors are the 31 monthly series of the four batches (sum form; cyclone energy is excluded because its final data lag months) over windows of 21, 63, 126 and 252 days. The targets are 110 instruments and the forward horizons 1, 5, 10, 21, 63, 126 and 252 trading days. A condition is the predictor in its upper or lower tail (10% or 20%, thresholds frozen at formation), and an event is the target's forward return in its upper or lower tail of the same size. A configuration is admitted on formation data alone, with outcomes purged at the 2012 cutoff, if it has at least 100 conditioned days, at least four episodes and a conditional probability at least 1.5 times the base rate. It is then scored on 2013-2026, with at least 100 conditioned days, as the ratio of its conditional probability to the test base rate, the out-of-sample lift; a configuration with no information scores one.

![Figure 6. Weather and climate predictors under one admission rule. Left, median out-of-sample lift by forward horizon on targets with a registered economic channel (10% tails). Right, mean lift by target group.](paper17_figs/fig_ladder.png)

Over all targets the arms differ in the direction the intuition expects: at 10% tails the 33,423 admitted climate configurations score a mean lift of 1.14 (median 0.97) and the 12,978 weather configurations 0.93 (median 0.84). The difference is not where a mechanism is (Table 2). On targets for which the registered prior has a channel for that arm (agriculture, energy, base metals, broad commodities, country equities and emerging-market currencies for climate; energy, energy names, sector equity, broad commodities and agriculture for weather), the arms are close: 1.10 for climate against 1.03 for weather at 10% tails (medians 0.98 and 0.95), and 1.06 against 1.10 at 20% tails (medians 0.98 and 1.06). On targets with no registered channel the climate lead is 1.16 against 0.87; that is where Treasuries, TIPS and credit sit, with climate lifts of 1.2 to 1.7, and no mechanism links an El Nino index to them, so the lead is a regime coincidence of the test period and not information.

| Targets | Climate, 10% tails | Weather, 10% tails | Climate, 20% tails | Weather, 20% tails |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| All | 1.14 (0.97), n = 33,423 | 0.93 (0.84), n = 12,978 | 1.05 (0.97), n = 26,626 | 0.92 (0.90), n = 5,792 |
| Registered channel | 1.10 (0.98), n = 11,547 | 1.03 (0.95), n = 4,661 | 1.06 (0.98), n = 9,876 | 1.10 (1.06), n = 2,000 |
| No registered channel | 1.16 (0.97), n = 21,876 | 0.87 (0.78), n = 8,317 | 1.05 (0.96), n = 16,750 | 0.83 (0.82), n = 3,792 |

*Table 2. Mean (median) out-of-sample lift of configurations admitted on formation data, by arm and target group. Overlapping configurations are not independent tests; counts are configurations.*

By forward horizon the weather predictors score below one at 1 to 21 days (medians 0.89 to 0.95), which is the noise result of Section 7 again, but the climate predictors do not improve with the horizon as the intuition expects: their median lift on registered targets is 1.10 at one day, 1.03 at 21, 0.93 at 63, 0.83 at 126 and 0.68 at 252 days. Within one variable the pattern is the same: degree-day anomalies over windows of up to 14 days score a mean lift of 1.07 on registered targets and windows of 63 to 252 days 0.97 (10% tails; 1.11 and 1.11 at 20%). The ladder therefore supports the first half of the thesis, that price moves in the days after a weather anomaly carry no persistent conditional structure, and does not support the second half in this form: once the targets are restricted to those with a mechanism, slow states are not distinguishable from weather by a general lift.

### 8.4 A causal walk-forward

A survivor filter uses the test period, so a survivor's test score is biased upward by construction. The causal walk-forward removes that. On 1 January of each year from 2016 to 2026, a candidate row (at least four formation episodes and the engine gates passed; 3,543 climate rows) is admitted if, using only 2013-onward observations whose forward outcome had already resolved before that date, it meets the survivor criteria: at least three episodes with a hit rate of two thirds or more, and a conditional probability at least ten points above the base rate on at least 100 firing days. Every episode entry (the first firing day) made that year by an admitted row is then scored: did the target's forward move land beyond its frozen threshold, against the base rate of that outcome over the same year? The program's own market predictors (2,723 rows) provide a positive control for the procedure.

![Figure 7. A causal walk-forward. Left, hit rate and base rate for entries by rows admitted from resolved outcomes only. Right, the decision rule of Section 8.5 as a paper portfolio on the climate extreme-tail entries, with 10 bp costs.](paper17_figs/fig_walkforward.png)

Admitted climate rows hit 55.8% against a 55.1% base over 534 entries, while rows not admitted hit 44.5% against 44.8% over 12,854. The persistence is in the tail. In the extreme tail (q_X of 0.90 or more, the reading registered in advance for the second and fourth batches) admitted rows hit 60.6% against 52.4% over 188 entries (168 distinct trades), a gain of 8.2 points; in the moderate tail they hit 53.2% against 56.5%, a loss of 3.3 points. The market control shows the same shape, larger: 69.4% against 54.6% over 111 extreme-tail entries (+14.8 points) and 67.8% against 60.1% overall. The extreme-tail gain is positive in three of four climate batches (observed extremes +9.9 points on 14 entries, the third batch +7.3 on 82, the fourth +11.0 on 81) and negative in the first (-8.0 on 11, the ENSO table). It sits in the weak-confidence tier (150 entries, +10.5 points), not in the standard tier whose mechanisms are best founded (38 entries, -1.0). It is concentrated: 121 of the 188 entries fall in 2020-2023. Measured as excess return per trade against the target's mean return in the entry year, the extreme-tail mean is +2.95% on a standard deviation of 16.4%, a ratio of 2.5 across entries and 0.9 across the eleven entry years, and 164 of the 188 entries are median-split direction bets (q_Y of 0.5), where a few points of hit rate buy little.

### 8.5 From rows to positions

The decision rule is the one the dashboard applies live (Section 9.4). An entry opens a vote on the target's direction from the next row until the row's horizon expires; opposite votes on a target cancel; the N targets with a vote are held at equal weight 1/N, rebalanced daily, at 10 bp per unit of turnover, with weekend rows rolled into the next weekday. Run on the causally admitted entries it returned -2.4% a year over all admitted rows (Sharpe -0.16; holding the same targets long: -3.5%), -1.4% a year in the extreme tail (Sharpe -0.08; holding the same targets long: +0.7%) and -2.4% in the moderate tail. The market control returned +14.2% a year (Sharpe 0.76) against +14.6% for holding the same targets long, and +4.7% in its extreme tail against +10.9%: its hit-rate edge reproduces long drift and adds nothing beyond it in this simple form.

Three things explain why a hit-rate edge does not become a return here. The excess is measured against a poor baseline: bearish extreme-tail entries lost 5.6% on average where unconditional shorts of the same targets in the same years lost 8.5%, so the raw mean return per trade is -0.1%. The holding periods are long (63 to 300 days for 97% of entries), the trades overlap, and the portfolio concentrates in a handful of targets (coffee, lean hogs, cotton, orange juice, sugar), so its volatility is high. And the entries are clustered in a few regimes, so the effective number of independent observations is far below the 188 entries.

### 8.6 The Sugar result and the policy mechanism

""" + sugar_sentence.replace("The sugar paper's own design was also run in the engine","The sugar paper's own design, the one climate link that was scored prospectively, was also run in the engine") + """

""" + xref(policy_par) + """

### 8.7 What the section supports

Four things survive scrutiny. Price moves in the days after a weather anomaly carry no persistent conditional structure, in two independent test designs (Sections 7.3 and 8.3). Slow climate states are not distinguishable from weather by a general out-of-sample lift once the targets are restricted to those with a registered mechanism. Rows admitted causally show persistence only in the rare extreme tail, as the program's own market predictors do, but the effect is small against trade-level variance, clustered in 2019-2023, concentrated in the weak-confidence tier and absent from a simple portfolio. And the ENSO bundle that dominates the survivor tables is one signal repeated across overlapping indices and a handful of episodes. The extreme, slow tail is therefore a candidate, and the test that can settle it is forward scoring of conditions frozen in advance, which Section 9 sets up."""
